<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Perfect Day! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> A Perfect Day!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend simple descriptions of people, places, and things around them. Interactive: Participate in call-and-response activities, responding appropriately to cues in songs or stories.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend essential weather-related words (e.g., "rainy" or "sunny"). Can follow simple descriptions of people in different weather conditions (e.g., "It's sunny. He is hot!"). Can participate in call-and-response songs, stories, and chants with appropriate cues (e.g., "How's the weather?" "It is rainy!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by greeting students and playing a simple, catchy "How's the Weather?" song (e.g., "What's the weather, what's the weather, what's the weather like today?"). The teacher models TPR actions for `sunny` (arms like a sun), `rainy` (wiggle fingers down), `cloudy` (arms in a cloud shape), and `windy` (waving arms side to side). Students listen and imitate the actions. This connects to the theme "A Perfect Day!" by introducing weather concepts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8 key vocabulary flashcards: `sunny`, `rainy`, `cloudy`, `windy`, `hot`, `cold`, `umbrella`, `coat`. For each card, the teacher holds it up, clearly pronounces the word, and models the pronunciation. Special attention is given to /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy", and phonemic awareness of the initial letter and sound of /w/ and final letter and sound /i/ in "windy". Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also models simple sentences like "It's a sunny day." and "I need my umbrella." using an adjective + noun structure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a `rainy` flashcard) "Is this sunny?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up an `umbrella` flashcard) "Is this an umbrella?" (Students respond: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to `cloudy` picture) "What's the weather like?" (Students respond: "Cloudy!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio story or dialogue, specifically designed for 2nd Grade Pre-A1.4 learners, accompanied by sequential weather picture cards. The audio will feature simple present tense sentences describing weather and activities, using target vocabulary (e.g., "It's a sunny day. I like sunny days. I play outside. Oh, now it's rainy. I need my umbrella and my coat. It's windy too, and it's cold!"). This is an authentic listening input.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend simple descriptions of people, places, and things around them. Pragmatic: Describing the weather and activities: "It's a sunny day, and I play outside." Sociolinguistic: Expressing preferences: Talking about preferences related to weather.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening - Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays the audio story. Students listen and point to the corresponding weather picture card as they hear the description (e.g., point to the `sunny` picture when they hear "It's a sunny day.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False - Listen and Thumbs Up/Down:</b> The teacher plays short phrases from the audio. After each phrase, the teacher makes a statement (e.g., Audio: "It's rainy. I need my umbrella." Teacher: "He needs his coat."). Students listen and give a 'thumbs up' for true or 'thumbs down' for false.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching - Listen and Choose:</b> Students have a worksheet with two pictures for each audio segment (e.g., a `hot` picture and a `cold` picture). They listen to a sentence (e.g., "It's a hot day!") and circle the correct picture.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening to and distinguishing specific weather vocabulary (`sunny`, `rainy`, `cloudy`, `windy`, `hot`, `cold`, `umbrella`, `coat`) and simple descriptions using adjectives + nouns (e.g., "rainy day"). They will also practice comprehending WH-questions like "What is the weather like?" and simple present simple sentences (e.g., "I like sunny days," "I need my coat"). Pronunciation focus on /s/, /r/, /w/, /i/ sounds remains.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - "Listen and Color":</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with outlines of various weather scenes and items (e.g., sun, cloud, umbrella, coat, child in rain). The teacher reads simple instructions: "Color the `sunny` day yellow." "Color the `umbrella` red." "Color the `coat` blue." Students listen carefully and color the designated items as instructed. This activity reinforces vocabulary recognition through listening.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - "Listen and Circle the Feeling":</b> The teacher plays short audio clips describing weather and a feeling (e.g., "It's a hot day. I feel hot!"). Students have a worksheet with two faces (happy/sad or hot/cold expressions). They listen and circle the face that matches the feeling described. The teacher explicitly uses `hot` and `cold` to reinforce understanding of temperatures and feelings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check - "Listen and Act":</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher calls out a weather word or a short description (e.g., "`Rainy`!," "It's `windy`!"). Students listen and perform the corresponding TPR action learned in the warm-up. The partner observes and provides a quick 'thumbs up' if the action is correct. This is a quick peer-check for listening comprehension and association.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Our Class Weather Report" (Call-and-Response Chant & Action)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher leads a whole-class "Weather Report" activity. The teacher displays large weather flashcards one by one (`sunny`, `rainy`, `cloudy`, `windy`, `hot`, `cold`). For each flashcard, the teacher asks the WH-question: "What's the weather like today?" Students listen to the question and then respond in chorus, using the target vocabulary with appropriate actions. For example, if the teacher shows a `sunny` flashcard, students listen to the question and respond: "It's `sunny`!" while doing the sun action. If the teacher shows `rainy`, they respond: "It's `rainy`! I need my `umbrella` and `coat`!" This connects directly to the scenario "All About the Weather" and allows students to participate actively and appropriately in call-and-response, aligning with "A Perfect Day!" through daily weather talk.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening and responding, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion by correctly identifying and responding to weather cues. Students are encouraged to use 1-3 word utterances (e.g., "It's sunny!", "Rainy day!").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students' ability to listen and correctly respond/identify during the "Our Class Weather Report" and "Listen and Match" tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Match" Worksheet</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a worksheet with three short audio descriptions (e.g., 1. "It's a sunny day, I like to play outside."; 2. "It's a rainy day, I need my umbrella and coat."; 3. "It's windy and cold!"). Next to these are scrambled pictures of a sunny park, a child with an umbrella, and a child bundled up against the wind. Students listen to each description and draw a line from the description to the matching picture. This assesses their comprehension of simple descriptions and essential weather-related words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify spoken weather words (e.g., "sunny," "rainy," "windy").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of simple descriptions (e.g., "It's a rainy day, I need my umbrella.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in call-and-response activities with appropriate cues.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct matching in the "Listen and Match" activity.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple card or asked verbally:
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I hear and understand 'sunny' and 'rainy'?" (Students draw a happy face or a sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I know what to do when I hear 'What's the weather like?'" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the students:
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new weather words did you hear today that you can remember?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which weather word or sound was easy for you to hear and understand?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What listening activities were most engaging for the 2nd Grade students? Did the TPR and visual aids effectively support comprehension at the Pre-A1.4 level?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the instructions clear and simple enough for all students to follow? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with distinguishing certain sounds like /w/ in "windy" or /i/ at the end of "windy"?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the foundational listening skills and vocabulary introduced in this lesson be scaffolded and integrated into reading activities for Lesson 2?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced core weather vocabulary and basic descriptions through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will build upon this by presenting simple illustrated texts (e.g., short sentences or a picture book page) that use the same weather vocabulary (`sunny`, `rainy`, `cloudy`, `windy`, `hot`, `cold`, `umbrella`, `coat`). Students will read these words and descriptions to identify main ideas and details, thus linking spoken comprehension to written recognition.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>